<ArticleLayout slug="automation-platform-or-production-partner" ctaLabel="Talk about the originate layer">
  <p class="standfirst">The comparison is usually framed as software against services, which is the wrong axis. The real question is who makes the thing being multiplied, and the automation layer answers that question in its own technical specification.</p>

  <h2>Do I need a creative automation platform or a production studio?</h2>

  <p>Both, almost always, and for different jobs. A creative automation platform assembles and serves variations of assets that already exist. It has no mechanism for originating one, which its own published standard makes explicit. A production studio such as hubStudio makes the master that the platform then multiplies. The decision is not which to buy, it is how much of your work is origination versus adaptation.</p>

  <p>Worth saying why this piece exists. Of the thirty-six results returned across four ways of asking this question, twenty-five were published by companies selling software or services in the category. Ask the question the other way around, "who makes the master creative", and the search returns nothing on topic at all. So the studio half of a two-sided question has simply never been answered in public.</p>

  <h2>What does a creative automation platform actually do?</h2>

  <p>Strip the marketing away and go to the standard. The category has a published technical specification, written by a standards body rather than by a vendor, and it is unusually clear about what the thing is.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A dynamic content ad is defined by its published standard as "a structured system of meta-data for creative components and asset variations of the creative components that may be used in an advertisement", with ad serving systems using that schema "to make real time decisions on which assets to use for a particular impression".</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: industry standards body, Dynamic Content Ad Specification, schema version 1.0, September 2016. Published technical standard, open repository.</p>

  <p>Read that carefully. Components, variations, and a decision about which existing asset to use. Every noun in the definition presupposes that the assets are already there.</p>

  <p>The trade body definition of the optimization layer says the same thing in plainer language.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A digital advertising trade body defines dynamic creative optimization as "the automated process of assembling and testing different combinations of ad elements (such as images, headlines and calls-to-action) in real time to show the most effective version of an ad to each user".</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national digital advertising trade body glossary, definition current September 2026, matched against the published technical standard.</p>

  <p>Assembling and testing combinations of elements. Not making elements.</p>

  <p>And the sequence is stated explicitly by the same trade body, which matters because it settles the order of operations rather than leaving it as a matter of opinion.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A trade body whitepaper describes the sequence plainly: creative optimization "only comes into play after two stages have been completed: employing dynamic creative to build multiple versions of an ad and then tailoring these ad variants for different audience segments".</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national digital advertising trade body, The Role of Creative in Digital Advertising Effectiveness, July 2018, council-authored whitepaper.</p>

  <p>Two stages have to be completed first. The platform is stage three.</p>

  <h2>Where does the automation layer's capability stop?</h2>

  <p>At the exact point where an asset does not exist. And the standard says what happens there, which is the most useful sentence in this entire argument.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same standard requires that every creative component define a fallback asset: "It is required to force the developer to define at least one fall back and not leave this undecided." An undefined component is a failure state to be caught, not a request for something new to be made.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: industry standards body, Dynamic Content Ad Specification, schema version 1.0, September 2016.</p>

  <p>That is the boundary, written into the specification by the people who defined the category. The automation layer has a defined behavior for a missing asset. The behavior is to substitute an asset that already exists. Nothing anywhere in the standard describes creating one, because creating one is not a thing the layer does.</p>

  <p>This is not a criticism. A fallback requirement is good engineering. It is simply a precise answer to the question of where the layer's capability ends, and it comes from the category's own documentation rather than from someone with a studio to sell.</p>

  <p>So the honest framing is not platform against studio. It is: something has to produce the components before the schema has anything to reference.</p>

  <h2>Does it matter how good the master is, if you can test enough variants?</h2>

  <p>Yes, and by a larger margin than the volume argument assumes. This is where the evidence is strongest, because it comes from measurement firms and peer-reviewed journals rather than from anyone selling either layer.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Advertising creative drives nearly half, 49 percent, of the incremental sales generated by advertising, ahead of brand at 21 percent, reach at 14 percent, targeting at 11 percent and recency at 5 percent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US advertising measurement study, August 2023, modeling the proportional contribution of 18 campaign features across nearly 450 US consumer packaged goods campaigns in digital and TV against purchase data.</p>

  <p>That is one study, so here is the same finding six years earlier on a different sample.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The earlier edition of the same study put creative at 47 percent of the impact on sales, with a further 2 percent for context, so the finding has held at roughly half of sales lift across six years and two samples.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US advertising measurement study, August 2017 edition, nearly 500 US studies from 2016 and 2017, restated by a national digital advertising trade body in July 2018.</p>

  <p>Roughly half, twice, across two samples six years apart. Targeting, the thing the automation layer exists to serve, comes in at 11 percent.</p>

  <p>The academic work says something more specific about what "good" means.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A meta-analysis of advertising creativity covering 93 data sets from 67 papers and 878 effect sizes found robust positive effects, and that both originality and appropriateness have to be present for creativity to pay.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Journal of Marketing, volume 84 issue 6, pages 39 to 56, 5 August 2020. Peer-reviewed meta-analysis with moderator tests.</p>

  <p>Both, not either. Original but wrong for the brand does not pay. Appropriate but unremarkable does not pay.</p>

  <p>And then there is the finding that connects creative quality directly to variant volume, which is the one that should change how a media plan is built.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In a controlled repetition experiment, ads high in divergence and relevance "wear in immediately and show little sign of wearing-out even over repeated exposures", while low-creativity ads followed "the classic inverted U-Shape" and declined with repeated exposure.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Journal of the Academy of Marketing Science, volume 44 issue 3, pages 334 to 349, 2016. Two-by-two-by-three between-subjects experiment across six dependent variables.</p>

  <p>Sit with the consequence. A strong master gets better with repetition. A weak one degrades. Multiplying a weak master into four hundred variants does not average the problem away, it buys reach for a curve that is bending downward. Volume is a multiplier, and it multiplies the sign of whatever it is applied to.</p>

  <p>One caveat, stated plainly because the temptation to overclaim here is real. No study compares templated output against originated output head to head. None of the above proves that a platform-assembled variant underperforms a studio-made one. What it establishes is narrower and still decisive: creative quality carries roughly half of advertising's sales effect, and quality is a property of the master.</p>

  <h2>Where is the handoff between the two layers?</h2>

  <p>At the component boundary defined in the standard. Everything upstream of a finished, approved component is origination. Everything downstream is assembly, versioning, targeting and serving.</p>

