  <p>The middle row is where the money actually is. Both layers are cheap at variant four hundred. Only one of them can produce variant zero, and variant zero is the one carrying roughly half the sales effect.</p>

  <p>Set against what a marketing budget has available:</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Production now takes an average of 19 percent of total marketing budgets, against 24 percent before the pandemic, while nearly two thirds of respondents expect to increase social media content production.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, survey of 50 global brands across more than 10 sectors, mostly senior marketing procurement respondents.</p>

  <p>Five points of budget share gone while output expectations rose. That squeeze is the reason the automation layer sells so well, and it is also the reason origination gets quietly under-resourced.</p>

  <h2>Do you already own the automation layer?</h2>

  <p>Probably, and that changes the question from "which do I buy" to "what is the gap in what I already run".</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">66 percent of brands now run an in-house agency and another 21 percent are actively considering one, a 16-point rise on 2020, while 56 percent expect to move more digital production in-house within three years.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body in-housing research, December 2023, survey of 45 multinational companies with an estimated combined annual global ad spend of $60 billion.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Inside those in-house teams, 65 percent are experimenting with AI and 71 percent are partially implementing it, 93 percent plan to invest further within 12 to 24 months, and just 12 percent describe themselves as fully integrated. The gains reported are throughput gains: 40 percent cite faster content production, 33 percent cite increased efficiency.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body research on in-house agencies, November 2025, quantitative survey plus 30 in-depth interviews with in-house agency leaders.</p>

  <p>Note what the reported gains are. Faster, more efficient. Throughput. Nobody reports that the tooling improved the idea, because that is not what tooling is for.</p>

  <p>Which lines up with the capability gap the same constituency reports.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">71 percent of marketers said their business was ambitious about delivering creative excellence, but only 37 percent felt their business had the right capabilities to deliver ground-breaking ideas.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: advertiser trade body and marketing intelligence service, September 2024, survey of more than 300 brand owners worldwide fielded January to February 2024.</p>

  <p>Ambition at 71, capability at 37. That gap does not close by adding a seat.</p>

  <h2>How do you decide, then?</h2>

  <p>On three axes. Volume, variation count, and the share of the work that is originated rather than adapted. The third is the one that decides it, and it is the one nobody asks.</p>

