  <h2>Questions buyers ask about platform against studio</h2>

  <h3>Can a creative automation platform replace a production studio?</h3>

  <p>No, and its own standard says why. The published specification for dynamic content ads requires every creative component to define a fallback asset, because the defined behavior when an asset is missing is to substitute one that exists. There is no mechanism in the standard for producing a new component, since production is not what the layer does.</p>

  <h3>What does creative automation actually do?</h3>

  <p>It assembles and serves combinations of creative elements that already exist, using a metadata schema so an ad server can choose which asset to show for a given impression. A trade body describes optimization as coming into play only after multiple versions have been built and tailored to segments, so assembly is stage three rather than stage one.</p>

  <h3>Who makes the master creative?</h3>

  <p>Whoever does direction, casting, capture or generation, and finishing. That is a production function whether it sits inside your company, inside an agency, or inside a studio. The automation layer consumes the output of that work and does not perform it.</p>

  <h3>Do I need both a platform and a studio?</h3>

  <p>Usually, and most brands already have the platform half. In December 2023, 66 percent of surveyed multinationals ran an in-house agency and 56 percent expected to move more digital production in-house within three years. The practical question is which half of the pipeline is currently under-resourced.</p>

  <h3>When is automation alone enough?</h3>

  <p>When your work is genuinely adaptation: a stable set of approved masters, versioned across sizes, languages and audiences, refreshed infrequently. If that describes your year, the origination requirement is small and buying it occasionally is reasonable.</p>

  <h3>Does creative quality really matter more than targeting?</h3>

  <p>On the available measurement, yes, by a wide margin. A US measurement study across nearly 450 consumer packaged goods campaigns attributed 49 percent of incremental sales to creative and 11 percent to targeting, and an earlier edition on a different sample put creative at 47 percent. Repetition research adds that strong creative resists wear-out while weak creative declines with exposure.</p>
